  "live_records": {
    "q-14507": {
      "dom_id": "step-q14507",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "A triangle has sides $x$, $y$, and $z.$ The sides $x$ and $z$ have lengths $3$ and $4$, respectively. If the angle $Y$ between $x$ and $z$ has a measure of ${139}^{∘}$, what is the length of side $y?$ Round your answer to two decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14269732/assets/7eaca5e621843857786ceda924f110804482d490c9dcd2615c6b3e50b89cde8e.png)\n\nThe Law of Cosines states that\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-2xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡Y,\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nm∠Y={139}^{∘},\\,x=3,\\,z=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the numbers into the formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+{4}^{2}-2(3)(4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡{139}^{∘} \\\\  & =9+16-24\\operatorname{cos}⁡{139}^{∘} \\\\  & =25-24(-0.754\\,70…) \\\\  & =25+18.113\\,03 \\\\  & =43.113\\,03\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nTaking the square root gives\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{43.113\\,03}=6.57\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
