    "q-14700": {
      "dom_id": "step-q14700",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14269732/assets/a1dcfc55b6e6139055822c4d8d30f33fc7280047de94f4c9636d99778a583b5a.png)\n\nFor the triangle above, find $m∠M$ in degrees to two decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "We use the Law of Cosines to solve for angle $M:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡M & =\\frac{{n}^{2}+{l}^{2}-{m}^{2}}{2nl}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHere, we have\n\n$$\nm=29,\\,n=20,\\,l=13.\n$$\n\nThis gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡M & =\\frac{{20}^{2}+{13}^{2}-{29}^{2}}{2(20)(13)} \\\\  & =\\frac{400+169-841}{520} \\\\  & =\\frac{-272}{520} \\\\  & =\\frac{-34}{65}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠M & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{34}{65}) \\\\  & ={121.54}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.",
