    "q-278856": {
      "dom_id": "step-q278856",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14269732/assets/8f72408d04931682377694800a59673c46321538bbcab22559a2eb07de690b29.png)\n\nRounded to the **nearest tenth of a degree**, $m∠B≈${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the Law of Cosines to solve for angle $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡B & =\\frac{{a}^{2}+{c}^{2}-{b}^{2}}{2ac}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHere, we have\n\n$$\na=9,\\,b=6,\\,c=7.\n$$\n\nThis gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡B & =\\frac{{9}^{2}+{7}^{2}-{6}^{2}}{2(9)(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{81+49-36}{126} \\\\  & =\\frac{94}{126} \\\\  & =\\frac{47}{63}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠B & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{47}{63}) \\\\  & ≈{41.8}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth.",
