    "q-278981": {
      "dom_id": "step-q278981",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "A triangle has sides $x$, $y$, and $z.$ The sides $x$, $y$, and $z$ are of lengths  $8$, $15$, and $17$, respectively. In degrees, what is the measure of the angle $X$ between the sides $y$ and $z?$ Round your answer to the **nearest tenth of a degree**.\n\n$m∠X≈$\n{{field-1}} ${}^{∘}$",
      "worked_solution": "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14269732/assets/db40512e5f82f1625602c6e8a350ebcf49c2d85596ae89e71b869a50f895315c.png)\n\nWe use the Law of Cosines to solve for angle $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡X & =\\frac{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{2yz}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nx=8,\\,y=15,\\,z=17.\n$$\n\nThis gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡X & =\\frac{{15}^{2}+{17}^{2}-{8}^{2}}{2(15)(17)} \\\\  & =\\frac{225+289-64}{510} \\\\  & =\\frac{450}{510} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠X & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{15}{17}) \\\\  & ≈{28.1}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
